<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">The ant walks, but the part of the rope under it stretches too.</p>
<svg viewBox="0 0 470 212" class="w-full" role="img" aria-labelledby="ant-rope-title ant-rope-desc">
<title id="ant-rope-title">Three stages of a rope growing as an ant progresses</title><desc id="ant-rope-desc">A rope expands in length; a marked ant is carried along as the entire rope stretches. Walking adds to its fraction of the rope crossed.</desc>
<g fill="none" stroke="#387a91" stroke-width="8" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M50 40 H170"/><path d="M50 105 H270"/><path d="M50 170 H440"/></g>
<g fill="#bd4b37" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"><circle cx="80" cy="40" r="9"/><circle cx="125" cy="105" r="9"/><circle cx="235" cy="170" r="9"/></g>
<g fill="currentColor" font-size="17"><text x="50" y="22">Earlier</text><text x="50" y="87">Later</text><text x="50" y="152">Later still</text><text x="194" y="43">Rope stretches</text><text x="294" y="108">The ant moves too</text></g></svg>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">The drawings are schematic snapshots, not measured positions. The rope behind the ant grows and carries it along. Each small walk adds a fraction of the rope’s length; those fractions accumulate until it reaches the far end, even though the rope grows faster than the ant walks.</figcaption></figure>